# Proxcloud PROD edge (ADR-0015 §3). The whole switch is: `active.caddy` is a
# symlink -> blue.caddy | green.caddy, flipped atomically by deploy.sh, then
# `caddy reload` (graceful, zero-drop; in-flight SSE/WS on the old color drain).
#
# ── TLS MODE — pick exactly ONE site block below (ADR-0015 §6) ───────────────
# pve01 sits behind a Cloudflare Tunnel, so Mode A (plain HTTP, TLS terminated
# at Cloudflare) is the likely prod path and is the DEFAULT here. To use a LAN
# internal CA or a public ACME cert instead, comment Mode A and uncomment B/C
# and set FRONTEND_ORIGIN in .env to match the scheme+host.
#
# NOTE: upstream/*.caddy force `header_up X-Forwarded-Proto https` so the backend
# marks the session cookie Secure. All three modes serve the site over HTTPS
# externally, so this constant is correct for A/B/C alike. Only change it (to
# `{http.request.scheme}`) if you deliberately serve the public site over plain
# HTTP — an unsupported, insecure configuration.

{
	admin localhost:2019
	# email you@example.com          # required for Mode C (ACME) only

	# ── Client-IP trust chain (ADR-0034) ────────────────────────────────────
	# Behind the Cloudflare Tunnel every request reaches Caddy from cloudflared,
	# so the TCP peer IP is useless as a client identity. Caddy must derive the
	# real client IP from CF-Connecting-IP — but ONLY when the connection comes
	# from cloudflared itself (trusted_proxies), or any direct client could
	# spoof it. The derived {client_ip} is what upstream/*.caddy writes into
	# X-Real-IP for the backend (which in turn trusts it only from
	# TRUSTED_PROXY_CIDRS and only when it parses as an IP).
	#
	# OPERATOR: set trusted_proxies to YOUR cloudflared source address. Both
	# common layouts, pick one:
	#   - cloudflared on the host, connecting via loopback (default below):
	#       trusted_proxies static 127.0.0.1/32 ::1/128
	#   - cloudflared as a container on the same docker network:
	#       trusted_proxies static 172.18.0.0/16   # its docker net/IP — find it:
	#       # docker network inspect proxcloud-edge -f '{{(index .IPAM.Config 0).Subnet}}'
	# If cloudflared's source is NOT listed here, {client_ip} falls back to the
	# TCP peer (cloudflared) — fail-safe: every user shares one bucket, nothing
	# becomes spoofable.
	servers {
		trusted_proxies static 127.0.0.1/32 ::1/128
		client_ip_headers CF-Connecting-IP
	}
}

# ── Mode A (DEFAULT): plain HTTP behind cloudflared; Cloudflare terminates TLS ─
# NOTE (flagged separately, operator decision): `:80` binds 0.0.0.0 — the origin
# is reachable from the LAN, not just via the tunnel. With cloudflared on the
# same host, `127.0.0.1:80` narrows the listener to loopback-only.
:80 {
	import /etc/caddy/upstream/active.caddy
}

# ── Mode B: LAN, Caddy internal CA ──
